    "abstract": "RLHF fits a reward model to human preferences and then fine-tunes a language model with RL to maximise it under a KL constraint. DPO uses a change of variables between reward functions and optimal policies to solve the same KL-constrained problem with a simple classification loss on preference pairs, with no reward model or sampling during training. DPO matches or exceeds PPO-based RLHF on sentiment control, summarisation, and single-turn dialogue.",
    "domain": "NLP",
    "key_claims": [
      "The optimal KL-constrained policy is pi*(y|x) ∝ pi_ref(y|x) exp(r(x,y)/beta), so reward can be written in terms of the policy.",
      "Substituting into the Bradley-Terry model yields a maximum-likelihood objective directly on the policy (Eq. 7).",
      "DPO's gradient weights each pair by how much the implicit reward model mis-orders it.",
      "DPO achieves a better reward/KL frontier than PPO on controlled sentiment generation and matches or improves win rates on TL;DR summarisation and Anthropic HH dialogue."
    ],
    "parsed_at": "2026-10-23T12:00:00+05:30",
